 is either a prime number or can be expressed as a product of prime numbers. A prime number is an integer greater than one that cannot be written as the product of two integers both greater than one. For example, 24 can be expressed as 2 × 12, and since 12 can be broken down further into 2 × 6, and 6 into 2 × 3, the complete factorization is 2 × 2 × 2 × 3. This process always terminates because the factors become smaller at each step until they are prime.

